 "figures": {
  "measures": {
   "title": "The floor, with each estate and without",
   "caption": "Each dot is one world at iteration 3,000, without the floor (grey: Experiment 19's ten worlds for parents as mates alone, Experiment 31's two for each estate) and with it (orange: Experiment 32's two for each). The dashed line is what the author's picture asks: every mark, a diversity score above B2's median world at 3,000 (0.54), a typical distance at least 1.5 times the random twin's, a Gini no lower than B2's most even world at 3,000 (0.47).",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** Experiment 32's six runs (`python3 gol_lab.py run E32`): B2 at 10,000 tokens with brains mixed at birth, parents as mates and a child never poorer than the median holding of its parent's neighbours, with the estate of the dead paid to the newborns (`B2-10000-12215f-\u2026`), to the parents (`-86df9e-\u2026`) or neither (`-b289dd-\u2026`), seeds 1 and 2, to 3,000 iterations; against Experiment 31's estate worlds of the same seeds (`B2-10000-06b876-\u2026`, `-b8e9fc-\u2026`) and Experiment 19's ten worlds (`B2-10000-bcc1bc-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), which have no floor.\n\n**Data.** From each world's archive at iteration 3,000.\n\n1. Read each world as `research/atlas/profile.py` reads every world: the seven marks, the four spreads ranked among the frozen reference worlds (their mean is the diversity score), the Gini of the tokens; and its typical distance over its random twin's, as `research/atlas/space.py` measures it (`research/atlas/readings.py` does both).\n2. The lines: every mark; the median diversity, and the least Gini, of B2's ten worlds played on and read at 3,000; a typical distance of 1.5 times the twin's.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py floor`."
  },
  "paupers": {
   "title": "Where the poor agents come from",
   "caption": "Left: the mean tokens of the agents of each age, in tens of iterations, at iteration 3,000, averaged over the worlds of each kind: Experiment 19's ten, and seeds 1 and 2 of the others. Right: each world's births an iteration and agents alive, both averaged over iterations 2,000 to 3,000; the dashed line is a hundred agents for every birth an iteration, which a world keeps when everyone lives out a hundred iterations.",
   "recipe": "**Runs.** Experiment 32's six runs (`python3 gol_lab.py run E32`): B2 at 10,000 tokens with brains mixed at birth, parents as mates and a child never poorer than the median holding of its parent's neighbours, with the estate of the dead paid to the newborns (`B2-10000-12215f-\u2026`), to the parents (`-86df9e-\u2026`) or neither (`-b289dd-\u2026`), seeds 1 and 2, to 3,000 iterations; against Experiment 31's estate worlds of the same seeds (`B2-10000-06b876-\u2026`, `-b8e9fc-\u2026`) and Experiment 19's ten worlds (`B2-10000-bcc1bc-s001` \u2026 `-s010`), which have no floor.\n\n**Data.** From each world's archive at iteration 3,000 and its `stats.jsonl`.\n\n1. Left: each agent's age is the iteration less the one it was born in; average the tokens of the agents aged 1 to 10, 11 to 20, and so on.\n2. Right: the births and the agents of each iteration's first phase, averaged over the iterations 2,000 to 2,999.\n\n**To make it again:** `python3 book_figures.py floor`."
  }
 }
}
